1
00:04:02,857 --> 00:04:08,857
See through another’s eyes, feel through another's heart,
and discover the meaning of brotherhood.

2
00:08:05,714 --> 00:08:11,714
Brother Bear is a adventure, animation, family film directed by Aaron Blaise.

3
00:12:08,571 --> 00:12:14,571
When an impulsive boy named Kenai is magically transformed into a bear, he must literally
walk in another's footsteps until he learns some valuable life lessons.

4
00:16:11,428 --> 00:16:17,428
His courageous and often zany journey introduces him to a forest full of wildlife, including the
lovable bear cub Koda, hilarious moose Rutt and Tuke, woolly mammoths and rambunctious rams.

5
00:20:14,285 --> 00:20:20,285
Kenai (voice), played by Joaquin Phoenix.

6
00:24:17,142 --> 00:24:23,142
Koda (voice), played by Jeremy Suarez.

7
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:26,000
Denahi (voice), played by Jason Raize.

8
00:32:22,857 --> 00:32:28,857
Rutt (voice), played by Rick Moranis.

9
00:36:25,714 --> 00:36:31,714
Tuke (voice), played by Dave Thomas.

10
00:40:28,571 --> 00:40:34,571
Sitka (voice), played by D. B. Sweeney.

11
00:44:31,428 --> 00:44:37,428
Brother Bear is a 2003 American animated musical comedy-drama
film produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation.

12
00:48:34,285 --> 00:48:40,285
It was directed by Aaron Blaise and Robert Walker, and written by Tab Murphy,
Lorne Cameron and David Hoselton, Steve Bencich and Ron J.

13
00:52:37,142 --> 00:52:43,142
The film features the voices of Joaquin Phoenix, Jeremy Suarez,
Rick Moranis, Dave Thomas, Jason Raize, and D.

14
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:46,000
The story follows a Native American boy named Kenai who is transformed into
a bear by the Great Spirits as punishment for killing one.

15
01:00:42,857 --> 01:00:48,857
To become human again, he must travel to a faraway mountain,
befriending a cub named Koda along the way.

16
01:04:45,714 --> 01:04:51,714
Brother Bear premiered at the New Amsterdam Theatre on October 20, 2003, and was
released in the United States on November 1 by Walt Disney Pictures.

17
01:08:48,571 --> 01:08:54,571
The film received mixed reviews from critics and grossed
$250 million against a $46 million budget.

18
01:12:51,428 --> 01:12:57,428
It received a nomination for Best Animated Feature at the 76th Academy Awards.

19
01:16:54,285 --> 01:17:00,285
A direct-to-video sequel, Brother Bear 2, was released on August 29, 2006.

20
01:20:57,142 --> 01:21:03,142
The film explores themes of friendship, sibling
relationship, transformation, grizzly bear, inuit.
